 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "MALHAM COVE. A curved cliff 80 metres high, made of limestone laid down when this was a warm tropical sea. After the Ice Age a waterfall as tall as Niagara poured over the lip. Today the beck vanishes underground and comes out at the bottom. Please do not follow it."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "LIMESTONE PAVEMENT. The cracks are called grykes and the blocks are clints. Ferns grow down in the grykes where it is sheltered. If you can identify a clint without a guidebook, you may call yourself a geologist."
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE YORKSHIRE DALES. Green valleys, grey walls, and a great many sheep with opinions. Welcome west. The tea shops are about two miles apart, which is the proper distance."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "A DRYSTONE WALL. Built with no mortar, only skill and a good eye. There are about 5,000 miles of them in the Dales, and every one is held up by gravity and pride. Please close the gate."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "AYSGARTH FALLS. Three sets of falls on the River Ure, stepping down over limestone ledges. This is where the film of the outlaw of Sherwood was shot in 1990. The Dales will play any forest if asked nicely."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "WENSLEYDALE. Famous for a crumbly white cheese, which a certain inventor with a dog loves. It goes very well with fruit cake. This is not a joke. It is a Yorkshire tradition."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "FIELD BARNS. The Dales have thousands of small stone barns, one to a field. Each kept hay for a few cows through the winter. Now they keep views for the walkers, and the occasional ewe."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "GORDALE SCAR. A gorge where the cliffs close in until they nearly touch overhead, and a waterfall comes down the middle. Turner painted it. John Ruskin said it was the best of its kind. The beck did not comment."
  }
 ],
